{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01FACGWFGJ23iafZvDeaM92j","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Verify Agent: Run Verification on a Candidate\n     2→\n     3→## Environment Setup\n     4→Before running any Python command, always activate the conda environment first:\n     5→```bash\n     6→conda activate wave\n     7→```\n     8→\n     9→## Your Role\n    10→You are a verification agent. Your task is to run the verification engine on a proposed candidate and produce a structured, detailed report.\n    11→\n    12→## Instructions\n    13→\n    14→1. **Run the verification engine** on the candidate file:\n    15→   ```bash\n    16→   conda activate wave && cd /local/home/cyanz/wave_PINN && python3 verify_engine.py /local/home/cyanz/wave_PINN/candidates/round_5/candidate.py\n    17→   ```\n    18→\n    19→2. **Also run the JSON output** for machine-readable results:\n    20→   ```bash\n    21→   conda activate wave && cd /local/home/cyanz/wave_PINN && python3 verify_engine.py /local/home/cyanz/wave_PINN/candidates/round_5/candidate.py --json\n    22→   ```\n    23→\n    24→3. **Read the candidate file** at `/local/home/cyanz/wave_PINN/candidates/round_5/candidate.py` to understand what ψ and parameters were proposed.\n    25→\n    26→4. **Read the candidate's reasoning log** at `/local/home/cyanz/wave_PINN/candidates/round_5/candidate_log.md` (if it exists) to understand the search agent's intent.\n    27→\n    28→5. **Write a structured verification report** to `/local/home/cyanz/wave_PINN/candidates/round_5/verification_report.md` with this exact format:\n    29→\n    30→```markdown\n    31→# Verification Report — Round 5\n    32→\n    33→## Candidate Summary\n    34→- psi = <expression>\n    35→- alpha = <value>\n    36→- s = <value>\n    37→- lambda = <value>\n    38→- Other params: <if any>\n    39→\n    40→## Condition Results\n    41→\n    42→### Necessary Condition 1: L₁ψ ≤ Lψ ≤ −L₁ψ\n    43→- Status: <True / False / unknown>\n    44→- Condition 1a (L − L₁ ≥ 0): <True / False / unknown>\n    45→- Condition 1b (−L₁ − L ≥ 0): <True / False / unknown>\n    46→- Symbolic expression (1a): <expression>\n    47→- Symbolic expression (1b): <expression>\n    48→- Numerical sampling: <result if applicable>\n    49→- Diagnosis: <why it fails or is unknown, what the dominant terms are>\n    50→\n    51→### Necessary Condition 2: lim(x→+∞) λψ = +∞\n    52→- Status: <True / False / unknown>\n    53→- λψ = <expression>\n    54→- Limit value: <value>\n    55→- Diagnosis: <explanation>\n    56→\n    57→### Necessary Condition 3: L₂ψ ≥ 0\n    58→- Status: <True / False / unknown>\n    59→- Symbolic expression: <expression>\n    60→- Numerical sampling: <result if applicable>\n    61→- Diagnosis: <why it fails or is unknown>\n    62→\n    63→### Sufficient Condition: (L₁ψ)² − (Lψ)² ≥ c²(φ_xt)²\n    64→- Status: <True / False / unknown>\n    65→- Symbolic expression: <expression>\n    66→- Numerical sampling: <result if applicable>\n    67→- Diagnosis: <explanation>\n    68→\n    69→## Overall Summary\n    70→- Necessary condition 1: <status>\n    71→- Necessary condition 2: <status>\n    72→- Necessary condition 3: <status>\n    73→- All necessary conditions: <status>\n    74→- Sufficient condition: <status>\n    75→- **ALL CONDITIONS PASS: <True / False>**\n    76→\n    77→## Failure Analysis\n    78→<For each failed or unknown condition, explain what went wrong and what\n    79→mathematical property the candidate would need to have to fix it.\n    80→This section is critical — the search agent will read it to improve\n    81→the next candidate.>\n    82→```\n    83→\n    84→IMPORTANT:\n    85→- Run the verification engine EXACTLY as specified. Do not modify verify_engine.py or the candidate file.\n    86→- If the engine crashes, report the error in the verification report.\n    87→- The failure analysis section is the most important part — it guides the next search iteration.\n    88→- Be precise about which terms dominate and why conditions fail.\n    89→- When showing that the candidate satisfy the sufficient condition, be extra careful, put your verification in solid math work that can pass all peer reviews.\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f1ef4913-77fb-452f-a75f-9074c083ece3","uuid":"9cc46f54-ceec-4b91-b422-9929e04f7565","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/local/home/cyanz/wave_PINN/candidates/round_5/prompt_verify.md","content":"# Verify Agent: Run Verification on a Candidate\n\n## Environment Setup\nBefore running any Python command, always activate the conda environment first:\n```bash\nconda activate wave\n```\n\n## Your Role\nYou are a verification agent. Your task is to run the verification engine on a proposed candidate and produce a structured, detailed report.\n\n## Instructions\n\n1. **Run the verification engine** on the candidate file:\n   ```bash\n   conda activate wave && cd /local/home/cyanz/wave_PINN && python3 verify_engine.py /local/home/cyanz/wave_PINN/candidates/round_5/candidate.py\n   ```\n\n2. **Also run the JSON output** for machine-readable results:\n   ```bash\n   conda activate wave && cd /local/home/cyanz/wave_PINN && python3 verify_engine.py /local/home/cyanz/wave_PINN/candidates/round_5/candidate.py --json\n   ```\n\n3. **Read the candidate file** at `/local/home/cyanz/wave_PINN/candidates/round_5/candidate.py` to understand what ψ and parameters were proposed.\n\n4. **Read the candidate's reasoning log** at `/local/home/cyanz/wave_PINN/candidates/round_5/candidate_log.md` (if it exists) to understand the search agent's intent.\n\n5. **Write a struc