{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01D7tu1jbvxyXZCy1FkS6ge1","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Verification Report — Round 4\n     2→\n     3→## Candidate Summary\n     4→- psi = -log(1+t) - log(1+x)\n     5→- alpha = 1\n     6→- s = -1/100\n     7→- lambda = -1/20\n     8→- Other params: none\n     9→\n    10→This is the same ψ as Rounds 2–3 but with |s| reduced from 1 to 1/100, intended to push the L₂ failure threshold further out.\n    11→\n    12→## Condition Results\n    13→\n    14→### Necessary Condition 1: L₁ψ ≤ Lψ ≤ −L₁ψ\n    15→- Status: **True** (proven analytically; engine reports \"unknown\" due to limitations of SymPy's automatic sign-checking)\n    16→- Condition 1a (L − L₁ ≥ 0): **True**\n    17→- Condition 1b (−L₁ − L ≥ 0): **True**\n    18→- Symbolic expression (1a): (9c²(1+t)² + 10(1+x)²) / (200·(1+t)^{39/20}·(1+x)^{39/20})\n    19→- Symbolic expression (1b): (10c²(1+t)² + 9(1+x)²) / (200·(1+t)^{39/20}·(1+x)^{39/20})\n    20→- Numerical sampling: 1a min = 5.69×10⁻⁴, 1b min = 5.15×10⁻⁴ (both positive across grid)\n    21→- Diagnosis: Both expressions have (i) a denominator that is always positive (product of positive powers of (1+t) and (1+x)), and (ii) a numerator that is a sum of two manifestly positive terms: a·c²(1+t)² + b·(1+x)² with a, b > 0. Specifically:\n    22→  - 1a numerator = 9c²(1+t)² + 10(1+x)² > 0 for all x ≥ 0, t ≥ 0, c > 0\n    23→  - 1b numerator = 10c²(1+t)² + 9(1+x)² > 0 for all x ≥ 0, t ≥ 0, c > 0\n    24→\n    25→  Each numerator is the sum of two squares multiplied by positive constants, so it is strictly positive everywhere. This does not depend on s, α, or λ.\n    26→\n    27→### Necessary Condition 2: lim(x→+∞) λψ = +∞\n    28→- Status: **True** (proven)\n    29→- λψ = (-1/20)·(-log(1+t) - log(1+x)) = (1/20)·log((1+t)(1+x))\n    30→- Limit value: +∞\n    31→- Diagnosis: As x → +∞, log(1+x) → +∞, so λψ = (1/20)·log((1+t)(1+x)) → +∞. This is immediate.\n    32→\n    33→### Necessary Condition 3: L₂ψ ≥ 0\n    34→- Status: **False** (L₂ < 0 for sufficiently large c)\n    35→- Symbolic expression: See full engine output. The L₂ numerator (after factoring out the always-positive denominator 80000000000·(1+t)^{79/20}·(1+x)^{79/20}) can be written in terms of z = c²(1+t)²/(1+x)² and R = ((1+t)(1+x))^{1/10} as:\n    36→\n    37→  N(z, R) = (1+x)⁴ · [A(z) − B(z)·R]\n    38→\n    39→  where:\n    40→  - A(z) = 5752500·z² + 95000·z + 5752500 (always positive: discriminant = 95000² − 4·5752500² ≈ −1.32×10¹⁴ < 0)\n    41→  - B(z) = 28z² + z − 29 = (z−1)(28z+29)\n    42→\n    43→- Numerical sampling (engine's small grid): min = 6.46×10⁻⁹ > 0 → \"likely_true\" on [0,50]×[0,10]\n    44→\n    45→- **Detailed diagnosis and proof of failure:**\n    46→\n    47→  **Case 1 (z ≤ 1):** B(z) = (z−1)(28z+29) ≤ 0, so −B(z)·R ≥ 0 and N = A(z) + |B(z)|·R > 0. ✓\n    48→\n    49→  **Case 2 (z > 1):** B(z) > 0, so we need A(z) ≥ B(z)·R, equivalently R ≤ f(z) where f(z) = A(z)/B(z).\n    50→\n    51→  The function f(z) for z > 1 has:\n    52→  - f(z) → +∞ as z → 1⁺ (denominator → 0)\n    53→  - f(z) → 5752500/28 = 205446.4 as z → ∞\n    54→  - A unique minimum at z* ≈ 212.07 where f(z*) ≈ 205437.1\n    55→\n    56→  In Case 2, the constraint z > 1 implies (1+x) < c(1+t) ≤ c(1+T), so:\n    57→  - (1+t)(1+x) < (1+T)·c(1+T) = c(1+T)²\n    58→  - R < (c(1+T)²)^{1/10} =: R_max(c,T)\n    59→\n    60→  For L₂ ≥ 0 in Case 2, we need R_max(c,T) ≤ f_min ≈ 205437.\n    61→\n    62→  **This requires c·(1+T)² < 205437¹⁰ ≈ 1.34 × 10⁵³.**\n    63→\n    64→  For c > 1.34 × 10⁵³ (with T of order 1), L₂ becomes negative. Concretely:\n    65→  - At c = 10⁵⁴, t = 0.5, x ≈ 1.03 × 10⁵³ (chosen so z ≈ z*): L₂ numerator ≈ −3.73 × 10⁹ < 0.\n    66→\n    67→  Since the problem requires the condition to hold for **all** c > 0, this candidate **fails** Condition 3.\n    68→\n    69→### Sufficient Condition: (L₁ψ)² − (Lψ)² ≥ c²(φ_xt)²\n    70→- Status: **True** (proven analytically; engine reports \"unknown\" due to SymPy limitations)\n    71→- Symbolic expression: The sufficient condition expression simplifies to:\n    72→\n    73→  3·(120c⁴(1+t)⁴ + 241c²(1+t)²(1+x)² + 120(1+x)⁴) / (160000·(1+t)^{39/10}·(1+x)^{39/10})\n    74→\n    75→- Denominator: always positive.\n    76→- Numerator: 3·(1+x)⁴ · (120u² + 241u + 120) where u = c²(1+t)²/(1+x)² ≥ 0.\n    77→- The quadratic Q(u) = 120u² + 241u + 120 has discriminant Δ = 241² − 4·120·120 = 58081 − 57600 = 481 > 0, but both roots are negative:\n    78→  - u₁ = (−241 + √481)/240 ≈ −0.913\n    79→  - u₂ = (−241 − √481)/240 ≈ −1.096\n    80→- Since Q(0) = 120 > 0 and both roots are negative, Q(u) > 0 for all u ≥ 0.\n    81→- Therefore the numerator is strictly positive for all x ≥ 0, t ≥ 0, c > 0. ✓\n    82→\n    83→- Numerical sampling: min = 2.93×10⁻⁷ > 0, consistent with the proof.\n    84→\n    85→## Overall Summary\n    86→- Necessary condition 1: **True** (proven: sum-of-positive-squares in numerator)\n    87→- Necessary condition 2: **True** (proven: logarithmic growth)\n    88→- Necessary condition 3: **False** (fails for c > ~10⁵³)\n    89→- All necessary conditions: **False**\n    90→- Sufficient condition: **True** (proven: quadratic with negative roots only)\n    91→- **ALL CONDITIONS PASS: False**\n    92→\n    93→## Failure Analysis\n    94→\n    95→### Only failing condition: L₂ ≥ 0 (Condition 3)\n    96→\n    97→**What fails:** The L₂ expression involves a competition between:\n    98→- A \"constant\" part A(z) = 5752500z² + 95000z + 5752500, which is always positive and does not grow with (1+t)(1+x).\n    99→- An \"R-growing\" part B(z)·R where R = ((1+t)(1+x))^{1/10} grows without bound.\n   100→\n   101→For z > 1 (which occurs when c(1+t) > (1+x)), the R-coefficient B(z) is positive. The ratio A(z)/B(z) has a minimum of ≈205437 over z > 1, so L₂ becomes negative when R exceeds this threshold.\n   102→\n   103→**Why the small-|s| strategy helps but doesn't fully solve it:** Reducing |s| from 1 (Rounds 2–3) to 1/100 (Round 4) increased the threshold from R_crit ≈ 20 to R_crit ≈ 205437 — a factor of ~10⁴ improvement. This pushed the failure from (1+t)(1+x) > 10¹³ to > 10⁵³. However, for the full problem (all c > 0), any fixed s still leads to failure at large enough c.\n   104→\n   105→**Why the candidate log's argument is incomplete:** The candidate log argues that on [0,∞) × [0,T] with fixed c, T, the region z > 1 has bounded R, so L₂ ≥ 0. This is correct for any **specific** (c,T). However, the problem demands conditions hold for **arbitrary** c > 0, meaning the same ψ and s must work for ALL c simultaneously. With s = −1/100 fixed, choosing c > ~10⁵³ (with T = 1) produces R_max > f_min, making L₂ negative.\n   106→\n   107→### Root cause\n   108→The fundamental issue is that the parameters (s, α, λ) are **constants** (not depending on c), while the L₂ expression has terms that grow with c. Specifically, in the z > 1 regime, R_max(c,T) = (c(1+T)²)^{1/10} grows as c^{1/10}, which eventually exceeds f_min for any fixed s.\n   109→\n   110→### How to fix it (guidance for next round)\n   111→\n   112→**Option A: Make s depend on c.** The problem explicitly allows s to depend on c and T. If s is chosen as s = −1/c^k for appropriate k > 0 (or s = −1/(c^a (1+T)^b)), then f_min scales as 1/|s|² → c^{2k}, while R_max scales as c^{1/10}. Choosing k > 1/20 would make f_min grow faster than R_max, ensuring L₂ ≥ 0 for all c.\n   113→\n   114→**Concrete suggestion:** Keep ψ = −log(1+t) − log(1+x), α = 1, λ = −1/20, but set:\n   115→- s = −1/(c² · (1+T)²)^{1/10}  (or equivalently s depending on c to ensure R_max < f_min)\n   116→\n   117→This would make the L₂ \"constant\" terms scale as 1/s² ∝ c^{2/5} while R_max scales as c^{1/10}, ensuring the constant terms dominate for all c.\n   118→\n   119→**Important caveat:** Verify that making s depend on c does not break Conditions 1, 2, or the Sufficient condition. For this ansatz, L, L₁, φ_xt do NOT depend on s (s only appears in L₂), so Conditions 1, 2, and the Sufficient condition remain unaffected.\n   120→\n   121→**Option B: Change the functional form of ψ.** Find a ψ for which L₂ is manifestly non-negative without requiring the constant-vs-R competition. For example, if the L₂ expression can be written as a sum of squares or as a product of non-negative factors, the condition would hold universally.\n   122→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f1ef4913-77fb-452f-a75f-9074c083ece3","uuid":"e69173f0-8765-4ad9-9e0a-13f33bf50071","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/local/home/cyanz/wave_PINN/candidates/round_4/verification_report.md","content":"# Verification Report — Round 4\n\n## Candidate Summary\n- psi = -log(1+t) - log(1+x)\n- alpha = 1\n- s = -1/100\n- lambda = -1/20\n- Other params: none\n\nThis is the same ψ as Rounds 2–3 but with |s| reduced from 1 to 1/100, intended to push the L₂ failure threshold further out.\n\n## Condition Results\n\n### Necessary Condition 1: L₁ψ ≤ Lψ ≤ −L₁ψ\n- Status: **True** (proven analytically; engine reports \"unknown\" due to limitations of SymPy's automatic sign-checking)\n- Condition 1a (L − L₁ ≥ 0): **True**\n- Condition 1b (−L₁ − L ≥ 0): **True**\n- Symbolic expression (1a): (9c²(1+t)² + 10(1+x)²) / (200·(1+t)^{39/20}·(1+x)^{39/20})\n- Symbolic expression (1b): (10c²(1+t)² + 9(1+x)²) / (200·(1+t)^{39/20}·(1+x)^{39/20})\n- Numerical sampling: 1a min = 5.69×10⁻⁴, 1b min = 5.15×10⁻⁴ (both positive across grid)\n- Diagnosis: Both expressions have (i) a denominator that is always positive (product of positive powers of (1+t) and (1+x)), and (ii) a numerator that is a sum of two